                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Demise of Nations is a turn-based grand strategy game where I take control of a nation and try to expand my territory, manage resources and outmanoeuvre opposing powers. The game focuses on long-term planning rather than fast reactions, so I have to think several turns ahead and consider how every military and economic decision could affect the rest of my campaign.\n\nThe biggest strength for me is the strategic freedom. I can decide whether to concentrate on building my economy, expanding aggressively or strengthening my existing territory. Different situations require different approaches, and I like that there is rarely one obvious way to handle a campaign. A strong position early on does not necessarily guarantee victory later.\n\nThe military system is also satisfying when everything comes together. I have to move units carefully, choose where to attack and think about defending important areas. Taking territory can be exciting, but expanding too quickly can leave my borders vulnerable, so I constantly have to balance aggression with caution.\n\nDiplomacy adds another useful layer. Other nations are not simply targets waiting to be conquered, and I have to consider alliances, rivalries and changing relationships. Negotiating with another faction can give me time to prepare for a larger conflict, although diplomatic decisions can also create problems later if I misjudge another nation's intentions.\n\nThe economic side is important as well. Military expansion requires resources, and I cannot simply build an enormous army without considering how it will affect the rest of my nation. Managing production and development gives peaceful turns a purpose and makes me think about the campaign beyond individual battles.\n\nThe biggest weakness is the learning curve. There are a lot of systems to understand, and the interface can feel overwhelming when I first start playing. It takes time to understand how the economy, military, diplomacy and territory systems interact, and some of the information is not always presented as clearly as I would like.\n\nThe presentation is functional rather than spectacular. The maps and interfaces provide the information I need, but there is not much visual excitement compared with more cinematic strategy games. For me, that is acceptable because the decisions are the real focus, although players looking for a visually impressive strategy experience may find it fairly plain.\n\nOverall, Demise of Nations is a deep turn-based strategy game that gives me plenty of freedom to shape a campaign. I enjoy the combination of military planning, economics, diplomacy and territorial expansion, and the slower pace gives my decisions real weight. The complicated systems and relatively plain presentation can make it difficult to approach, but once I understand the mechanics, there is a lot of strategic depth to explore.\n\nRating: 8/10\n",
